              Safety
            </span>
            <span class="text-gray-400 text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
              <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>
              15 min read
            </span>
          </div>
          <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-heading font-bold mb-6 leading-tight">
            S2 lab requirements in Germany: what is mandatory and what you can skip
          </h1>
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 mb-8 leading-relaxed">
            Floors, basin, safety cabinet, autoclave, ventilation and the registration with the authority: what GenTSV and the Biological Agents Ordinance require for S1 and S2 labs, with legal references and a checklist. And which costly extras no rule asks for.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-2 text-sm text-gray-400">
            <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="18" height="18" x="3" y="4" rx="2" ry="2"/><line x1="16" x2="16" y1="2" y2="6"/><line x1="8" x2="8" y1="2" y2="6"/><line x1="3" x2="21" y1="10" y2="10"/></svg>
              <time datetime="2026-10-22">Updated 22 October 2026</time>
            </span>
            <a href="/en/team/thomas-boss" class="flex items-center gap-2 hover:text-primary">
              <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H9a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"/><circle cx="12" cy="7" r="4"/></svg>
              Thomas Boss, Technical Director, Planexus
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-gray-100 py-4">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500">
          <a href="/en" class="hover:text-primary">Home</a>
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="9 18 15 12 9 6"/></svg>
          <a href="/en/magazine" class="hover:text-primary">Magazine</a>
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="9 18 15 12 9 6"/></svg>
          <span class="text-[#5c7300] font-medium">S2 lab requirements</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <article class="py-16">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-12">
          <div class="lg:col-span-8 min-w-0">
            <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

              <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                You are planning a lab for cell culture, microbiology or genetic engineering in Germany, and the safety officer's first question is: S1 or S2? The answer decides which floor goes down, where the basin goes, whether a safety cabinet has to fit into the room and whether you wait 45 days for the authority.
              </p>

              <div class="bg-slate-50 border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-2xl p-6 my-8 not-prose">
                <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-[#5c7300] font-bold mb-2">In short</p>
                <p class="text-gray-800 leading-relaxed">
                  An <strong class="text-slate-900">S2 laboratory</strong> is a lab at safety level 2 under German genetic engineering law or protection level 2 under the Biological Agents Ordinance, where work carries a low risk. It needs liquid-tight surfaces down to the wall-floor junction, a basin by the door, an eye wash, a safety cabinet where aerosols may form and an autoclave in the same building. An airlock, negative pressure and HEPA filters are not required.
                </p>
              </div>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The rules are spread across the Genetic Engineering Act, the Genetic Engineering Safety Ordinance, the Biological Agents Ordinance and several technical rules. Search for them and you mostly find authority PDFs, some of them more than 20 years old. This article sets out the current position, every requirement with its legal reference. It is written by Thomas Boss, Technical Director at Planexus, who designs and builds lab containers for pharma, biotech and research, so it also covers what the rules mean on site. S1 and S2 broadly correspond to BSL-1 and BSL-2, but the details below follow German law.
              </p>

              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8 not-prose">
                <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Contents</p>
                <ol class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-700 list-decimal list-inside">
                  <li><a href="#what-is-s2" class={linkClass}>What is an S2 laboratory?</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#s1-lab" class={linkClass}>What are the requirements for an S1 laboratory?</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#s2-lab" class={linkClass}>What are the design requirements for an S2 laboratory?</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#not-required" class={linkClass}>What an S2 laboratory does not need</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#ventilation" class={linkClass}>What ventilation does an S2 laboratory need?</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#registration" class={linkClass}>Does an S2 laboratory need a permit?</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#upgrade" class={linkClass}>From S1 to S2</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#mistakes" class={linkClass}>Five planning mistakes</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#staff" class={linkClass}>Staff and maternity protection</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#dach" class={linkClass}>Switzerland and Austria</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#container" class={linkClass}>An S2 lab as a lab container</a></li>
                  <li><a href="#checklist" class={linkClass}>Checklist</a></li>
                </ol>
              </div>

              <h2 id="what-is-s2" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">What is an S2 laboratory?</h2>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                In German labs, "S2" refers to two different things, and many labs fall under both. <strong class="text-slate-900">Genetic engineering law</strong> (GenTG and GenTSV) classifies work with genetically modified organisms into safety levels 1 to 4. The <strong class="text-slate-900">Biological Agents Ordinance</strong> assigns bacteria, viruses, fungi and cell cultures to risk groups 1 to 4 and derives protection levels for laboratories from them. If you handle genetically modified bacteria of risk group 2, you have to meet both. For planning, the stricter requirement applies in each case.
              </p>

              <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 not-prose">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-200 min-w-[560px]">
                  <caption class="text-left text-sm text-gray-500 pb-2">Safety levels and risk groups compared</caption>
                  <thead class="bg-slate-100">
                    <tr>
                      <th class="text-left p-3 border border-gray-200 text-slate-900">Level</th>
                      <th class="text-left p-3 border border-gray-200 text-slate-900">Genetic engineering (Section 7 GenTG)</th>
                      <th class="text-left p-3 border border-gray-200 text-slate-900">Biological agents (BioStoffV)</th>
                      <th class="text-left p-3 border border-gray-200 text-slate-900">Typical example</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">1</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">no risk expected</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">unlikely to cause human disease</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">Cloning in E. coli K12</td></tr>
                    <tr class="bg-primary/5"><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">2</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">low risk</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">can cause disease, spread unlikely, usually treatable</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">Staphylococcus aureus, lentiviral vectors</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">3</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">moderate risk</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">severe disease, spread possible</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">Mycobacterium tuberculosis</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">4</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">high risk</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">severe disease, high risk of spread</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">Ebola virus</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The examples are for orientation. What binds you is the classification of your specific work: for organisms in the statements of the Central Committee on Biological Safety (ZKBS), for biological agents in the technical rules TRBA 460 to 468. Where in doubt, Section 7(1a) GenTG assigns the higher level. What is added from level 3 is described in our article <a href="/en/magazine/bsl-2-bsl-3-container-lab" class={linkClass}>BSL-2 and BSL-3 in a container</a>.
              </p>

              <h2 id="s1-lab" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">What are the requirements for an S1 laboratory?</h2>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                An S1 lab is quickly described. Under Annex 2 of the GenTSV it needs signage as a level 1 genetic engineering work area, work surfaces that are easy to clean and resist disinfectants, a basin with soap and towel dispensers, doors with a vision panel and an autoclave somewhere on the site. Windows should be shut during work, and lab coats hang apart from street clothes.
              </p>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                A properly equipped standard lab usually meets this already. If S2 work is likely to follow within two or three years, build the floor and work surfaces to S2 quality straight away. Replacing a floor covering in a running lab costs many times what the better finish costs during construction.
              </p>

              <h2 id="s2-lab" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">What are the design requirements for an S2 laboratory?</h2>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                An S2 lab needs liquid-tight surfaces right into the corners, a basin by the door, an eye wash, no floor drains, a safety cabinet for anything that forms aerosols and an autoclave in the same building. The table sets the GenTSV (Annex 2, Part A, Section II) beside the Biological Agents Ordinance (Annex II, protection level 2). "Mandatory" and "recommended" are the ordinance's own terms, and recommended measures must be implemented where they reduce the risk (Section 10 BioStoffV).
              </p>

              <figure class="my-10 not-prose">
                <Picture
                  src="/images/blog-s2-labor-anforderungen.jpg"
                  alt="Illustrative image of an S2 laboratory in a lab container: safety cabinet on the left, seamless grey floor coved up the wall, stainless steel basin with elbow lever, dispensers and eye wash next to the door with vision panel and biohazard sign, autoclave and incubator behind"
                  width={1448}
                  height={1086}
                  loading="lazy"
                  class="w-full h-auto rounded-2xl shadow-lg"
                />
                <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-3 text-center italic">
                  Illustrative image: an S2 lab in a container. Safety cabinet in a quiet spot, basin with dispensers and eye wash by the door, biohazard sign, seamless coved floor, autoclave in the room.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 not-prose">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-200 min-w-[620px]">
                  <caption class="text-left text-sm text-gray-500 pb-2">Design and technical requirements for an S2 laboratory</caption>
                  <thead class="bg-slate-100">
                    <tr>
                      <th class="text-left p-3 border border-gray-200 text-slate-900">Element</th>
                      <th class="text-left p-3 border border-gray-200 text-slate-900">GenTSV, safety level 2</th>
                      <th class="text-left p-3 border border-gray-200 text-slate-900">BioStoffV, protection level 2</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Floor, walls, work surfaces</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">liquid-tight, including adjacent walls and the wall-floor junction</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">impermeable, easy to clean, disinfectant-resistant: mandatory</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Hand-wash basin</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">near the door, with disinfectant, soap and towel dispensers, hands-free tap where possible</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">washing and decontamination facility: mandatory</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Eye wash</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">must be available</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">per risk assessment</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Drains</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">no floor drains, sinks with a raised rim</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">no specific rule</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Safety cabinet</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">aerosols must not enter the room</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">where aerosols form: mandatory</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Autoclave</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">in the facility or in the same building</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">own autoclave recommended, defined inactivation mandatory</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Process exhaust air</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">filtered or thermally treated before release</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">not into the work area: mandatory</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Door</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">vision panel, opens in direction of escape, level 2 and biohazard signage</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">vision panel mandatory, protection level and biohazard symbol on the door</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Windows</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">closed during work</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">may open, but shut during work</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Access</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">only those involved and persons authorised by the project leader</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">named employees, access control for listed pathogens</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Waste</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">tightly closed, labelled containers</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">inactivate before disposal unless a specialist contractor disposes of it</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Emergency power</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">no rule</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">recommended for safety-related equipment</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                On paper this looks manageable. In planning, there are three points where projects keep getting stuck.
              </p>

              <h3 class="text-xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-4">The floor has to run up the wall</h3>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                At S2, "liquid-tight" does not stop at the edge of the floor. The GenTSV explicitly names the adjacent walls and the wall-floor junction, and according to a trade article on baulinks.de, DGUV Information 213-086 also requires watertight floor coverings from S2. In construction terms that means seamless flooring coved up the wall, welded seams, sealed penetrations and lab benches that join wall and floor tightly with no voids behind them. Before approving samples, check which disinfectant your hygiene plan uses. Not every floor covering tolerates every product for years.
              </p>

              <h3 class="text-xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-4">The basin belongs by the door, the drain does not belong in the floor</h3>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Hands are washed and disinfected on the way out, before anyone touches the handle. So the basin sits next to the door, ideally with an elbow lever or sensor, with the eye wash right beside it. Floor drains are not intended in the S2 area, and sinks in work surfaces need a raised rim. For the floor plan that means grouping all water points on one wall and agreeing the waste water route with the installation site early.
              </p>

              <h3 class="text-xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-8 mb-4">The safety cabinet gets the quietest spot</h3>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Pipetting, vortexing, centrifuging and opening culture vessels all form aerosols. At S2 that makes a microbiological safety cabinet mandatory, usually Class II to DIN EN 12469. The air curtain at its front opening is sensitive: a door, a walkway or a supply air outlet nearby can disturb it. Fix the cabinet's position on the floor plan first, then plan the door, supply air and walkways around it. If hazardous substances are used in the same room, a fume cupboard is added. What it has to achieve is covered in our article on <a href="/en/magazine/lab-container-fume-hood-din-en-14175" class={linkClass}>fume cupboards to DIN EN 14175</a>.
              </p>

              <h2 id="not-required" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">What an S2 laboratory does not need</h2>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                An airlock, negative pressure and HEPA filters sound like safety, so they often end up in the room programme for an S2 lab. None of them is required. Annex II of the Biological Agents Ordinance explicitly lists the following as <strong class="text-slate-900">not required</strong> for protection level 2:
              </p>

              <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-600 mb-6">
                <li>an airlock with interlocking doors,</li>
                <li>permanent negative pressure in the lab area,</li>
                <li>HEPA filters for room supply and exhaust air,</li>
                <li>a room that can be sealed for fumigation,</li>
                <li>showers before leaving the area,</li>
                <li>windows that cannot be opened.</li>
              </ul>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The GenTSV also reserves these measures for level 3. Each one costs floor space, plant and maintenance, and an airlock adds time to every trip into the lab. My advice is to plan an S2 lab as an S2 lab. Anyone who later moves to S3 work needs an authorisation and permit with their own requirements anyway, and an "S2 plus" with airlock and negative pressure does not replace that.
              </p>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Two extras do make sense. A small anteroom for changing makes the required separation of protective and street clothing easier and separates the area from the rest of the building, which the ordinance recommends. Emergency power for the safety cabinet and refrigeration, also recommended, protects samples that represent months of work. Beyond that, no rule normally asks for more at S2. If the risk assessment demands more for a particular activity, the risk assessment applies.
              </p>

              <h2 id="ventilation" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">What ventilation does an S2 laboratory need?</h2>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                There is no prescribed air change rate for S2 itself. The requirement almost always comes from hazardous substances law, because hardly any biological lab works without ethanol, formaldehyde or disinfectants. <strong class="text-slate-900">TRGS 526 "Laboratories"</strong> then applies, with a reference value of <strong class="text-slate-900">25 m³ of air per hour per square metre</strong> of lab floor. At a 3-metre ceiling that is a little over eight air changes per hour. A lower rate is only possible if the risk assessment supports it permanently, and then work with larger quantities of volatile or flammable substances is restricted and the door must carry a notice about the reduced air change rate.
              </p>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The S2 rule "windows shut during work" makes mechanical ventilation practically indispensable. A full S2 room with safety cabinet, incubators, centrifuges and fridges gets very warm in summer. Ventilation and cooling therefore belong in one concept, together with the fume cupboard and cabinet exhaust. How we handle this in a container is described in our article on <a href="/en/magazine/lab-container-hvac-ventilation-standards" class={linkClass}>HVAC in lab containers</a>. Slight negative pressure towards the corridor is allowed and keeps odours in the lab. It should stay weak enough that opening the door does not send a draught through the safety cabinet.
              </p>

              <h2 id="registration" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">Does an S2 laboratory need a permit?</h2>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                No. For a level 2 genetic engineering facility, a <strong class="text-slate-900">registration</strong> (Anmeldung) with the state authority is enough (Section 8(2) GenTG). Work may begin <strong class="text-slate-900">45 days after receipt</strong>, or earlier with the authority's consent (Section 12(5) GenTG). A permit is only required from level 3.
              </p>

              <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 not-prose">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-200 min-w-[560px]">
                  <caption class="text-left text-sm text-gray-500 pb-2">Procedures under the Genetic Engineering Act and the Biological Agents Ordinance</caption>
                  <thead class="bg-slate-100">
                    <tr>
                      <th class="text-left p-3 border border-gray-200 text-slate-900">Case</th>
                      <th class="text-left p-3 border border-gray-200 text-slate-900">Procedure</th>
                      <th class="text-left p-3 border border-gray-200 text-slate-900">Start of work</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody class="text-gray-700">
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">S1 facility, first work</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">notification, "Anzeige" (Section 8(2) GenTG)</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">immediately after receipt (Section 12(5a))</td></tr>
                    <tr class="bg-primary/5"><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">S2 facility, first work</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">registration, "Anmeldung" (Section 8(2) GenTG), permit on request</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">after 45 days, earlier with consent (Section 12(5))</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Further S2 work in a registered facility</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">notification (Section 9 GenTG)</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">immediately after receipt</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">S3 or S4 facility</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">facility permit (Section 8(1) GenTG)</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">once granted</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">First targeted activity with risk group 2 agents</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">notification to the occupational safety authority (Section 16 BioStoffV)</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">at least 30 days in advance</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200 font-semibold">Protection level 3 or 4</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">authorisation (Section 15 BioStoffV)</td><td class="p-3 border border-gray-200">once granted</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The 45 days can grow longer. The period is suspended while the authority waits for documents it has requested or obtains a ZKBS statement. The latter happens for work that has not yet been classified in a comparable case (Section 12(4) GenTG). If the authority has not responded by the end of the period, that counts as consent. The biggest lever on your schedule is therefore a complete registration first time: site plan, floor plan, facility description, risk assessment, safety measures, project leader, biological safety officer and waste disposal (Section 12(2) with Section 10 GenTG). The federal states provide the forms, and depending on the state the competent body is the regional council, the district government or a state office.
              </p>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Building law runs separately. A new building, a conversion with a change of use or an installed lab container may need planning permission, which the genetic engineering procedure does not replace. Both procedures can be prepared in parallel once the floor plan and equipment are fixed. What to expect on the building side is covered in our guide to <a href="/en/magazine/lab-container-permits-building-law" class={linkClass}>lab container permits</a>.
              </p>

              <h2 id="upgrade" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">How does an S1 lab become an S2 lab?</h2>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                If an existing S1 lab is to host S2 work, the facility changes substantially. For substantial changes at levels 1 and 2, the rules on notification and registration apply accordingly (Section 8(4) GenTG). In practice you register the facility for level 2 and wait the 45 days before the first S2 work begins.
              </p>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Before that, walk the lab with the table above in hand. Typical weak spots in an S1 lab are these: the floor meets the wall with a skirting board instead of a cove, the basin sits in the middle of the room instead of by the door, an old floor drain is still open and there is no eye wash. Signage, dispensers and access rules are quick to add. The floor and water points mean building work in a running lab, and that is when a <a href="/en/magazine/temporary-lab-renovation-rental-containers" class="text-[#5c7300] font-medium hover:underline">temporary lab in a container</a> is often faster than converting room by room.
              </p>

              <h2 id="mistakes" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">Five planning mistakes that cost time on an S2 lab</h2>

              <ol class="space-y-4 text-gray-700 mb-8 not-prose">
                <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold shrink-0">1.</span> <span><strong class="text-slate-900">Classification comes after the floor plan.</strong> If you plan first and classify later, you build for the wrong level. Activities and organisms belong at the start.</span></li>
                <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold shrink-0">2.</span> <span><strong class="text-slate-900">The safety cabinet stands next to the door.</strong> Everyone entering then disturbs its air curtain, and moving it later often means new connections.</span></li>
                <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold shrink-0">3.</span> <span><strong class="text-slate-900">The autoclave question stays open.</strong> If there is none in the same building, you need space, power, water and heat removal for your own unit, which shifts the whole floor plan.</span></li>
                <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold shrink-0">4.</span> <span><strong class="text-slate-900">Ventilation is sized by floor area instead of heat load.</strong> 25 m³ per hour per square metre covers air quality, but not automatically the heat from safety cabinet, incubators and fridges.</span></li>
                <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold shrink-0">5.</span> <span><strong class="text-slate-900">The registration goes out incomplete.</strong> Every request for more documents stops the 45-day clock. More on typical delays in our article <a href="/en/magazine/when-the-new-lab-arrives-too-late-5-planning-mistakes" class="text-[#5c7300] font-medium hover:underline">When the new lab arrives too late</a>.</span></li>
              </ol>

              <h2 id="staff" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">Staff and maternity protection</h2>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Anyone doing genetic engineering work appoints a project leader and a biological safety officer or committee (Section 6(4) GenTG). Operating instructions, training, a hygiene plan and records come on top. TRBA 100 was reissued in November 2025 and now covers training and external contractors in more detail, which also affects tradespeople and service technicians working in the S2 area.
              </p>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Many lab safety briefings say pregnant employees must not enter the S2 lab. The law is more precise. Section 11(2) of the Maternity Protection Act prohibits work in which a pregnant woman could come into contact with biological agents of risk groups 2 to 4 in a way that poses an unjustifiable risk to her or her child. Rubella virus and Toxoplasma are named explicitly, and with sufficient immunity that risk is deemed excluded. Whether an employee can keep working in the S2 lab is therefore decided by the updated risk assessment for her specific tasks.
              </p>

              <h2 id="dach" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">Switzerland and Austria</h2>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                In <strong class="text-slate-900">Switzerland</strong> the Containment Ordinance (ESV) applies, with classes 1 to 4. Class 1 and 2 activities are notified via the ECOGEN portal to the federal Biotechnology Contact Point and may begin once the notification has been received. Classes 3 and 4 need authorisation, and occupational safety is governed by the SAMV. In <strong class="text-slate-900">Austria</strong> the Genetic Engineering Act (GTG) and the Biological Agents Ordinance (VbA) apply, with their own notification and approval routes. Clarify procedures and deadlines there directly with the authority. Regional notes for <a href="/en/lab-containers-zurich" class={linkClass}>Zurich</a>, <a href="/en/lab-containers-basel" class={linkClass}>Basel</a> and <a href="/en/lab-containers-vienna" class={linkClass}>Vienna</a> are on our location pages.
              </p>

              <h2 id="container" class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-6">An S2 lab as a lab container: weeks instead of years</h2>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Lab space for life science companies is scarce in Munich and Berlin. The Munich cluster association BioM reports that demand for lab space there exceeds supply, and a provider of fully equipped labs quoted in that report names S2 labs as particularly short. A lab container creates the space where it is needed: on your own site, next to the institute, at the hospital.
              </p>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The rules regulate surfaces, equipment, ventilation, signage and organisation. They do not regulate the construction method. A <a href="/en/lab-containers" class={linkClass}>lab container</a> can therefore be registered as an S1 or S2 facility. The tricky S2 details are even easier in the factory than on a building site, because coving, welded seams, penetrations and benches are installed in the hall and checked before delivery. Planexus builds S1 and S2 containers at its plant in Haigerloch, and a custom-planned container is typically ready in <strong class="text-slate-900">8 to 14 weeks</strong>.
              </p>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                This is how manufacturing and registration fit together:
              </p>

              <ol class="space-y-4 text-gray-700 mb-8 not-prose">
                <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold shrink-0">1.</span> <span><strong class="text-slate-900">Room programme and classification:</strong> activities, equipment, level under GenTG and protection level under BioStoffV. The autoclave question is settled here too.</span></li>
                <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold shrink-0">2.</span> <span><strong class="text-slate-900">Floor plan, safety cabinet first:</strong> Planexus supplies the floor plan, equipment list and material details in a form that goes straight into your registration documents.</span></li>
                <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold shrink-0">3.</span> <span><strong class="text-slate-900">Submit the registration, start manufacturing:</strong> the authority's 45 days run while the container is built. In parallel you clarify planning permission, foundations and connections.</span></li>
                <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold shrink-0">4.</span> <span><strong class="text-slate-900">Install, connect, test the safety cabinet:</strong> then training and start of work. A project in detail is shown in our article on the <a href="/en/magazine/lab-container-project-process" class={linkClass}>project process</a>.</span></li>
              </ol>

              <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8 my-10 not-prose">
                <p class="text-xl font-heading font-bold text-primary mb-3">Clarify your S2 room programme with us</p>
                <p class="text-gray-300 mb-5 leading-relaxed">Send us your equipment list or a sketch. In a free initial consultation we work through floor space, cabinet position, autoclave, ventilation and the documents your registration needs.</p>
                <a href="/en/contact" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-primary text-slate-900 font-bold px-6 py-3 rounded-lg hover:bg-primary/90 transition-colors">Request an S2 lab consultation
                  <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14"/><path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7"/></svg>
                </a>
              </div>

              <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                If you need the lab for a limited period, for instance during a refurbishment or until a new building is ready, a <a href="/en/rent-lab-containers" class={linkClass}>rental lab container</a> is the right fit. Our article on the <a href="/en/magazine/temporary-lab-renovation-rental-containers" class={linkClass}>temporary lab during renovation</a> shows how this works. For a permanent extension, <a href="/en/buy-lab-containers" class={linkClass}>buying</a> pays off. And if you need a compact unit quickly, the <a href="/en/lab-containers/labtogo" class={linkClass}>LABtoGO</a> is a lab container with an S2-capable lab room. Research groups in <a href="/en/lab-containers-munich" class={linkClass}>Munich</a>, <a href="/en/lab-containers-berlin" class={linkClass}>Berlin</a> and elsewhere will find matching solutions on our pages for <a href="/en/industries/pharma-biotech" class={linkClass}>pharma and biotech</a> and <a href="/en/industries/research-universities" class={linkClass}>research and universities</a>.
              </p>

              <div id="checklist" class="bg-slate-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8 my-8 not-prose">
                <h2 class="text-xl font-heading font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Checklist: planning an S2 laboratory</h2>
                <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-700">
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Classify the work: safety level under GenTG, protection level under BioStoffV, or both</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Appoint a project leader and a biological safety officer</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Seamless floor coved up the wall, work surfaces and wall joints liquid-tight</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Choose the disinfectant first, then approve material samples</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Basin with dispensers by the door, eye wash beside it</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>No floor drains, sinks with a raised rim</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Class II safety cabinet in the quietest spot</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Autoclave in the same building or in the container, inactivation defined</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Plan ventilation under TRGS 526 and equipment cooling load together</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Door with vision panel, level 2 and biohazard signage, restricted access</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Submit the Section 8 GenTG registration and Section 16 BioStoffV notification complete</span></li>
                  <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <span>Clarify planning permission in parallel</span></li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-5 items-start bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-10 not-prose">
                <div class="shrink-0 w-14 h-14 rounded-full bg-slate-900 text-primary flex items-center justify-center font-heading font-bold text-lg" aria-hidden="true">TB</div>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-slate-900">About the author</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm leading-relaxed mt-1">
                    <a href="/en/team/thomas-boss" class={linkClass}>Thomas Boss</a> is shareholder and Technical Director of Planexus GmbH in Albstadt. He is responsible for planning, building services and the turnkey delivery of lab containers for pharma, biotech, research, hospitals and crisis teams in Germany, Switzerland and Austria.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
                Sources: Genetic Engineering Act Sections 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12; Genetic Engineering Safety Ordinance Annex 2 Part A, version of 12 August 2019 (BGBl. I p. 1235); Biological Agents Ordinance Sections 10, 15, 16 and Annex II; Maternity Protection Act Section 11 (all gesetze-im-internet.de); TRBA 100, November 2025 edition (GMBl. of 10 November 2025, amended 19 November 2025); TRGS 526 "Laboratories"; DGUV Information 213-086 as reported by baulinks.de on 14 February 2022; Swiss Federal Office of Public Health (FOPH) on notifications under the Containment Ordinance; BioM, "Available laboratory space in Munich". This article is not legal advice and does not replace a risk assessment. As of 22 October 2026.
              </p>

            </div>
          </div>

          <aside class="lg:col-span-4">
            <div class="sticky top-28 space-y-8">
              <div class="bg-slate-50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-gray-100">
                <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">S2 at a glance</p>
                <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-700">
                  <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold">→</span> Registration rather than permit, start after 45 days</li>
                  <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold">→</span> Seamless floor, coved up the wall</li>
                  <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold">→</span> Basin by the door, no floor drain</li>
                  <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold">→</span> Safety cabinet for aerosols, autoclave in the building</li>
                  <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold">→</span> Ventilation: 25 m³/(h·m²) under TRGS 526</li>
                  <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#5c7300] font-bold">→</span> Not required: airlock, negative pressure, HEPA</li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-100 p-6 rounded-2xl">
                <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Further reading</p>
                <ul class="space-y-3">
                  {furtherLinks.map((l) => (
                    <li><a href={l.href} class="flex items-center gap-2 text-gray-700 hover:text-primary transition-colors text-sm"><Fragment set:html={chevron} />{l.label}</a></li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>

              <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white p-6 rounded-2xl">
                <p class="font-bold mb-3">Planning an S2 lab?</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-4">We work through cabinet position, autoclave, ventilation and the documents for your registration with you.</p>
